<Base title="Indexing queries: diagnosis and patterns" description="Why a query ignores an index you expected it to use, how to read the index lines of a plan in PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite and SQL Server, and the index each common query shape needs: a sorted list with a limit, the latest row per group, joins and foreign keys, queues, soft deletes, case-insensitive lookups and search screens with optional filters.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header">
   <p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p>
   <h1>Indexing queries: diagnosis and patterns</h1>
   <TitleLine type="Companion" version="PostgreSQL 18, with InnoDB (MySQL 8.4), SQLite and SQL Server comparisons" />
  </header>
<ArticleBody>

  <p><a href={indexes}>Indexes</a> explains how a B-tree finds rows, and why a condition can narrow the scan, be checked inside the index, or be checked only at the table. This article applies that to real queries. It starts with the question most readers arrive with, “why isn’t my index used?”, and then works through the query shapes that recur in almost every application.</p>
  <p>Every failure here is quiet. The query still returns the right rows; it just reads the whole table, or far more of an index than it should. The plan is where it shows, so the article starts with how each engine reports what an index did. To find which queries are slow in the first place, see <a href="/learn/databases/relational/slow-queries/">Slow queries</a>.</p>
  <p>The examples use the 60,000-order table from Indexes, plus a <code>customers</code> table, 60,000 accounts with email addresses and a job queue. PostgreSQL 18 is the source of every page count. Where a number came from the Indexes experiment, the SQL names the table <code>index_orders</code>, as that script does. The <a href="#run">last section</a> says which runs produced them.</p>

  <section id="plans">
   <h2>Reading a plan in four engines</h2>
   <p>Each engine reports the same few facts in its own words. The questions in the left column are the ones that matter for an index.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="plans-caption"><table><caption id="plans-caption">Where four engines report what an index did</caption><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>PostgreSQL <code>EXPLAIN</code></th><th>MySQL 8.4 <code>EXPLAIN</code></th><th>SQLite <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code></th><th>SQL Server plan</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Was the whole table read?</td><td><code>Seq Scan</code></td><td><code>type</code> is <code>ALL</code></td><td><code>SCAN t</code></td><td><code>Table Scan</code> or <code>Clustered Index Scan</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Was the whole index read?</td><td>Not named: compare the pages read with the index’s size</td><td><code>type</code> is <code>index</code></td><td><code>SCAN t USING INDEX i</code></td><td><code>Index Scan</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Was an index searched?</td><td><code>Index Scan</code>, <code>Index Only Scan</code>, <code>Bitmap Index Scan</code></td><td><code>type</code> is <code>ref</code>, <code>range</code>, <code>eq_ref</code> or <code>const</code>, with the index in <code>key</code></td><td><code>SEARCH t USING INDEX i (a=?)</code></td><td><code>Index Seek</code> or <code>Clustered Index Seek</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Which conditions narrowed the search?</td><td>The leading ones under <code>Index Cond</code></td><td>The <code>key_len</code> shows how many key columns were used</td><td>The terms in parentheses</td><td><code>Seek Predicates</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Which were checked afterwards?</td><td>Later columns under <code>Index Cond</code>; columns outside the index under <code>Filter</code></td><td><code>Using index condition</code> in the index; <code>Using where</code> on the row</td><td>Not shown</td><td><code>Predicate</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Did the index cover the query?</td><td><code>Index Only Scan</code>, with <code>Heap Fetches</code></td><td><code>Using index</code> in <code>Extra</code></td><td><code>USING COVERING INDEX</code></td><td>No <code>Key Lookup</code> or <code>RID Lookup</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Did the query sort?</td><td><code>Sort</code>, <code>Incremental Sort</code></td><td><code>Using filesort</code></td><td><code>USE TEMP B-TREE FOR ORDER BY</code></td><td><code>Sort</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>How much was read?</td><td><code>Buffers</code>, in pages</td><td>No pages; <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> gives rows and loops</td><td>Not reported</td><td>Logical reads, from <code>SET STATISTICS IO ON</code></td></tr>
   </tbody></table></div>
   <p>MySQL has one more counter worth knowing. Run <code>FLUSH STATUS</code>, then the query, then <code>SHOW SESSION STATUS LIKE 'Handler_read%'</code>, and the counts show how many index entries and rows the query read.</p>
   <p>In every engine the decisive comparison is rows read against rows returned. A plan that names the right index but reads thousands of entries to return ten is not working, whatever its wording says.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnosis">
   <h2>Why an index goes unused</h2>
   <p>Two complaints sound alike. In one, the plan scans the whole table although an index exists. In the other, the plan names the index, yet the query reads far more than it should. Both start the same way: read the plan, and compare the rows the planner expected with the rows it got.</p>
   <p>Then work through the causes below; <a href="#prove">Proving the index can be used</a> puts the checks in order. The table maps what a plan shows to the likely cause.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="diagnosis-caption"><table><caption id="diagnosis-caption">From what the plan shows to the likely cause</caption><thead><tr><th>The plan shows</th><th>Likely cause</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>A full scan, with a function, arithmetic or cast on the column in the filter</td><td><a href="#bare-column">The column has to stand alone</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>A full scan, with a conversion you didn’t write (<code>CONVERT_IMPLICIT</code>, <code>::numeric</code>)</td><td><a href="#types">Types and collations have to agree</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>A full scan on a <code>LIKE</code> pattern</td><td><a href="#like">Prefix searches with LIKE</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>An index read from start to end, with a condition on a later column only</td><td><a href="#leading">The leading column has no condition</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>A full scan on conditions joined by <code>OR</code></td><td><a href="#or">An OR with an unindexed branch</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>A full scan, and the condition matches a large share of the table</td><td><a href="#too-much">The condition matches too much</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Estimated rows far from actual rows</td><td><a href="#stale">Statistics that no longer match the data</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>A good plan when you test, a bad one in the application</td><td><a href="#parameters">Plans made for parameters</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>An index scan on the sort column with a huge <code>Rows Removed by Filter</code></td><td><a href="#limit-trap">The planner walks the wrong index</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>The index is not mentioned at all, and it should apply</td><td>It may be invalid, invisible or disabled: <a href="/learn/databases/relational/maintaining-indexes/#drop-invalid">Invalid indexes</a></td></tr>
   </tbody></table></div>
   <p>A very small table belongs on the list too. A table of a few pages is cheaper to scan than to search, and the planner is right to scan it.</p>

   <h3 id="bare-column">The column has to stand alone</h3>
   <p>An index holds the stored values of its column, in order. A condition can use it only when the database can turn the condition into a position or range in that order, which means the bare column on one side and a value on the other. With an index on <code>customer_id</code> and another on <code>placed_at</code>:</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="sargable-caption"><table><caption id="sargable-caption">The same question written so an index can and cannot answer it (PostgreSQL 18, 60,000 orders)</caption><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Pages read</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><code>placed_at &gt;= '2026-02-01' AND placed_at &lt; '2026-02-02'</code></td><td>49</td><td>A range of the stored values</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>date_trunc('day', placed_at) = '2026-02-01'</code></td><td>1,725</td><td>The function’s result is not in the index</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>customer_id = 42</code></td><td>62</td><td>The stored value</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>customer_id + 0 = 42</code></td><td>1,725</td><td>Arithmetic on the column</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>customer_id::text = '42'</code></td><td>1,725</td><td>A cast of the column</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>customer_id = 42::numeric</code></td><td>1,725</td><td>The integer column is cast up to numeric</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>customer_id = 42::bigint</code></td><td>62</td><td>Integer and bigint compare without a cast</td></tr>
   </tbody></table></div>
   <p>The cure is to move the work to the other side. Compare the column with a half-open range instead of truncating it, and give a value the column’s own type. When the expression is the point, as in a case-insensitive match, index the expression and write the query with exactly that expression, as <a href={`${indexes}#expression`}>Expression indexes</a> shows.</p>
   <p>Negation is a related case. <code>customer_id &lt;&gt; 42</code> is two ranges at either end of the index, and it keeps almost every row anyway, so it scanned all 1,725 pages. When the query wants the rare rows, ask for them directly, or index only them with a <a href="#queue">partial index</a>.</p>

   <h3 id="types">Types and collations have to agree</h3>
   <p>A comparison between two types makes the database convert one side. If the conversion lands on the indexed column, the index is out of the picture, and nobody wrote the cast: a driver or an ORM chose the parameter’s type. The engines convert in different directions.</p>
   <ul>
    <li><strong>PostgreSQL</strong> refuses to compare a text column with an integer, so its silent case is between number types: a driver that sends every number as <code>numeric</code> forces an integer column up, as the table showed.</li>
    <li><strong>MySQL</strong> can’t use the index on a string column compared with a number, because the number 1 equals many strings, <code>'1'</code>, <code>' 1'</code> and <code>'01'</code> among them. Joining two string columns with different character sets, such as <code>utf8mb4</code> and <code>latin1</code>, also rules out the index. The plan shows <code>type: ALL</code> on the inner table.</li>
    <li><strong>SQL Server</strong> converts the side with the lower data type precedence. Under the common <code>SQL_</code> collations, an <code>nvarchar</code> parameter against a <code>varchar</code> column converts the column, and the plan shows <code>CONVERT_IMPLICIT</code> and an index scan where a seek was expected. Microsoft’s JDBC driver and .NET’s <code>AddWithValue</code> send strings as <code>nvarchar</code> unless told otherwise. Declare the parameter as <code>varchar</code>, or turn off the JDBC driver’s <code>send<wbr>String<wbr>Parameters<wbr>As<wbr>Unicode</code> property.</li>
    <li><strong>SQLite</strong> applies the column’s affinity to the value, so a <code>TEXT</code> column compared with the number 42 still searched its index in our test.</li>
   </ul>
   <p>For joins, the rule MySQL’s manual states holds in every engine: declare joined columns with the same type, size and, for strings, character set and collation.</p>

   <h3 id="like">Prefix searches with LIKE</h3>
   <p><code>WHERE email LIKE 'ab1%'</code> should be a range: every string from <code>ab1</code> up to <code>ab2</code>. Whether the database can make it one depends on how the index orders strings. A language collation may ignore case and punctuation on a first pass, so strings sharing a prefix aren’t guaranteed to sit together; a character-by-character order such as <code>"C"</code> is.</p>
   <p>In a PostgreSQL database with an English collation, an ordinary index on 60,000 accounts was ignored for <code>LIKE 'ab1%'</code>, 442 pages. An index declared with <code>text_pattern_ops</code> or <code>COLLATE "C"</code> read 16. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/indexes/#like">Indexes in PostgreSQL</a> compares the options, and trigram indexes for <code>'%ab1%'</code>.</p>
   <p>MySQL treats a <code>LIKE</code> as a range when the pattern is a constant that doesn’t start with a wildcard, and the index’s collation decides case. SQLite’s <code>LIKE</code> ignores ASCII case by default, so it uses an index only if the index is declared <code>COLLATE NOCASE</code>; an ordinary index was ignored in our test. SQL Server can seek on a constant prefix in the column’s collation. In every engine a pattern that starts with <code>%</code> has no range, and needs a full-text or trigram index instead.</p>

   <h3 id="leading">The leading column has no condition</h3>
   <p>An index on <code>(a, b)</code> doesn’t help a query on <code>b</code> alone, but the plan can still name it, which makes the problem easy to miss. Figure 1 takes the small index from Indexes’ range-scan figure and gives it a second key column, <code>placed_at</code>, and asks it for five days of orders from every customer. Watch where the search starts.</p>
   <LeadingFigure number={1} />
   <p>With no <code>customer_id</code> to look for, the search had nowhere to descend to. It started at the first leaf page and tested every entry, because each customer’s dates are sorted only within that customer’s run. Given a customer as well, it went straight to one leaf page.</p>
   <p>On the 60,000-order table, with only <code>(customer_id, placed_at)</code> in place, a 30-minute window read every leaf page of the index, 234 pages, for 30 rows. Its plan still showed <code>placed_at</code> under <code>Index Cond</code>. A plain index on <code>placed_at</code> found them in 5. The giveaway is the page count, not the wording. MySQL reports this as <code>type: index</code> and SQLite as <code>SCAN ... USING INDEX</code>; SQL Server shows an <code>Index Scan</code>.</p>

   <h3 id="or">An OR with an unindexed branch</h3>
   <p>An <code>OR</code> across two columns needs an index for every branch, because the database must find each branch’s rows separately and merge them. <code>customer_id = 42 OR total = 19.99</code>, with no index on <code>total</code>, read all 1,725 pages. With both branches indexed, the engine can search each and merge the results, as <a href={`${indexes}#combining`}>Combining indexes</a> describes.</p>
   <p>When the planner misjudges the combined condition, write the branches as separate queries joined by <code>UNION</code>, so each uses its own best index:</p>
   <Sql code={unionQuery} label="Each branch as its own search, joined by UNION" />
   <p>An <code>OR</code> on one column, <code>customer_id = 42 OR customer_id = 43</code>, is an <code>IN</code> list, and every engine searches it value by value.</p>

   <h3 id="too-much">The condition matches too much</h3>
   <p>Four in five orders are paid, and with an index on <code>status</code> the query for paid orders was still a sequential scan of 1,725 pages. To see what the index would have done, turn off the routes the planner preferred, in your own session only. In PostgreSQL, <code>SET enable_seqscan = off</code> and <code>SET enable_bitmapscan = off</code> make those routes look very expensive.</p>
   <p>The forced index scan read 1,426 pages, a sixth fewer, but in an order the planner priced as mostly random, so it judged the scan cheaper. Whether the forced plan is faster depends on your storage, which is what <code>random_page_cost</code> describes; compare times on your own server.</p>
   <p>The other engines have hints for the same experiment. MySQL has the optimizer hint <code>INDEX</code> and the older <code>FORCE INDEX</code>, which it plans to deprecate. SQLite has <code>INDEXED BY name</code> and <code>NOT INDEXED</code>. SQL Server has the table hints <code>WITH (INDEX(name))</code> and <code>WITH (FORCESEEK)</code>. Core PostgreSQL has none. Use them to learn, and fix the cause rather than leaving a hint in production code.</p>

   <h3 id="stale">Statistics that no longer match the data</h3>
   <p>The planner chooses by estimated rows, and statistics collected before a large change can put the estimate far out. An <code>events</code> table held 50,000 <code>done</code> rows when its statistics were collected, then 40,000 <code>new</code> rows arrived. Asked for <code>kind = 'new'</code>, the plan estimated 1 row and returned 40,000.</p>
   <p>That plan still turned out well: the index on <code>kind</code> read 723 pages against 1,552 for a full scan, because the new rows sat together at the end. A join built on the same estimate did worse. Expecting one row, the planner chose a nested loop into a <code>deliveries</code> table: 40,000 index searches and 120,723 pages. After <code>ANALYZE</code>, it chose a hash join and read 1,565.</p>
   <p>A gap of ten times or more between estimated and actual rows is the sign to look for, and it does most harm where the estimate feeds a later choice, such as the join method. <a href={`${execution}#refresh`}>How a query runs</a> explains when each engine refreshes its statistics, and why a bulk load should end with <code>ANALYZE</code>.</p>

   <h3 id="parameters">Plans made for parameters</h3>
   <p>A query you test with a literal value can get a different plan in the application, which sends a parameter. The plan may be made once and reused for every value, so it can’t depend on the value.</p>
   <ul>
    <li><strong>PostgreSQL</strong> makes a custom plan for each of a prepared statement’s first five executions, then switches to a generic plan if that doesn’t look costlier on average. A generic plan can’t use a partial index for <code>state = $1</code>, because it can’t know <code>$1</code> will be <code>'queued'</code>. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/query-execution/#parameters">Planning and executing a PostgreSQL query</a> covers <code>plan_cache_mode</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>SQL Server</strong> compiles the plan for the first values it sees, known as parameter sniffing, and reuses it. A plan sniffed for a rare value can be wrong for a common one. A filtered index isn’t used for a condition on a parameter or local variable at all, because the value is unknown at compile time. <code>OPTION (RECOMPILE)</code> compiles for the actual values, at the price of a compile every time.</li>
   </ul>
   <p>So test with the parameter mode the application uses. The search screen with optional filters, the worst case of this, is the <a href="#optional-filters">last pattern</a> below.</p>

   <h3 id="prove">Proving the index can be used</h3>
   <p>Before deciding the planner is wrong, rule out the plain explanations in order:</p>
   <ol>
    <li>Check that the index exists and is usable: valid in PostgreSQL, visible in MySQL (<code>SHOW INDEX</code> has a <code>Visible</code> column), not disabled in SQL Server (<code>is_disabled</code> in <code>sys.indexes</code>).</li>
    <li>Check that the condition has a form the index can serve, as the cases above describe.</li>
    <li>Compare estimated with actual rows.</li>
    <li>Force the index in your own session and compare the pages or time of the two plans.</li>
   </ol>
   <p>If the forced plan is clearly better and the statistics are fresh, the cost settings may not suit your storage, which <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/indexes/#cost">Indexes in PostgreSQL</a> covers. Judge any fix on production-shaped data, as <a href="/learn/databases/relational/slow-queries/#verification">Testing an improvement</a> advises.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="order-limit">
   <h2>A sorted list with a limit</h2>
   <p>The commonest query in an application is a list: the rows for one owner, newest first, one screenful at a time.</p>
   <Sql code={orderList} label="The order list for one customer" />

   <h3 id="rule">When an index supplies the order</h3>
   <p>An index on <code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC)</code> holds customer 42’s entries as one run, already newest first. The database reads 20 entries and stops, and the other 40 of customer 42’s orders are never fetched.</p>
   <Sql code={orderIndex} label="An index whose entries run in the order the list needs" />
   <p>The list then read 23 pages, against 62 with an index on <code>customer_id</code> alone.</p>

   <h3 id="breaks">What stops the index supplying the order</h3>
   <p><a href={`${indexes}#order`}>Indexes</a> gives the rules for direction, NULLs and collation. One more case catches people: a column in the middle with no equality. With an index on <code>(customer_id, status, placed_at DESC)</code>, the order list, which never mentions <code>status</code>, read 63 pages and sorted, because customer 42’s entries are split by status. If queries don’t always name the middle column, move it after the sort column, <code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC, status)</code>, which keeps the order and still checks <code>status</code> inside the index (26 pages with a status filter).</p>
   <p>A range before the sort column breaks the order the same way: equality columns first, then the sort column, and any range on a later column is checked as the scan goes.</p>

   <h3 id="limit-trap">When the planner walks the wrong index</h3>
   <p>With an <code>ORDER BY</code> and a small <code>LIMIT</code>, the planner has a tempting option: walk an index on the sort column in order, check each row against the filter, and stop at the first match. It works when matching rows are spread evenly, and it fails badly when they aren’t.</p>
   <p>With separate indexes on <code>customer_id</code> and <code>placed_at</code>, asking for customer 42’s latest order walked the <code>placed_at</code> index from the newest end. Customer 42 owns one order in a thousand, so the planner expected a match soon, and it came after 959 rows were thrown away: 33 pages.</p>
   <p>Then 60 orders were added for a new customer, 1001, all dated December 2025, older than every other order. The same query for customer 1001 took the same plan:</p>
   <Sql code={latestQuiet} label="The latest order of a customer whose orders are all old" />
   <p>It walked the whole index, discarded 60,000 rows and read 1,892 pages, more than a full scan. The planner’s estimate assumes the matches are spread through the sort order; it can’t know they sit at the far end. A composite index removes the gamble:</p>
   <Sql code={latestIndex} label="The filter column first, then the sort column" />
   <p>With it, both customers’ latest orders read 4 pages. A plan showing an index scan on the sort column with a large <code>Rows Removed by Filter</code> is this trap. MySQL’s optimizer makes the same bet when a <code>LIMIT</code> is present, governed by its <code>prefer_ordering_index</code> switch.</p>
   <p id="tenants">Multi-tenant tables meet the trap often, because tenants differ so much in size. In a tenants’ events table where one tenant owned half the rows, an index on <code>created_at</code> alone found the large tenant’s newest 20 in 3 pages and a small tenant’s in 210. With <code>(tenant_id, created_at DESC)</code>, the small tenant took 24. The tenant column belongs first in almost every index of such a table.</p>

   <h3 id="grouping">The latest row per group</h3>
   <p>The same ordered index gives one customer’s newest order in a single descent, so “each customer’s newest order” can ask for it once per customer:</p>
   <Sql code={latestPerCustomer} label="The newest order of each customer, one index descent per customer" />
   <p>With <code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC, order_id DESC)</code>, the 1,000 customers cost 3,008 pages, about three each, and the five customers in the <code>north</code> region cost 23. Without that index, the five cost 8,632. PostgreSQL’s <code>DISTINCT ON (customer_id)</code>, with the same ordering, reads the index once in order and keeps the first entry of each customer: 299 pages for all 1,000. Use <code>DISTINCT ON</code> when most groups are wanted and the lateral form when only some are. MySQL supports <code>LATERAL</code> but not <code>DISTINCT ON</code>; SQL Server writes the lateral form as <code>CROSS APPLY</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="joins">
   <h2>Joins and foreign keys</h2>

   <h3 id="join-side">Which column needs the index</h3>
   <p>Of the three join methods <a href={execution}>How a query runs</a> compares, only the nested loop wants an index: it takes each row from the outer side and searches the inner side for matches. So the index goes on the inner side’s join column. Looking up a customer from an order uses the customer’s primary key, already indexed. Looking up orders from a customer uses <code>orders.customer_id</code>, which is indexed only if someone created the index.</p>
   <Sql code={northJoin} label="Five customers in one region with their orders" />
   <p>Without an index on <code>orders.customer_id</code>, PostgreSQL hashed the five customers and scanned all of orders, 1,732 pages. With it, a nested loop read 317. A join that touches most rows is better served by a hash join whatever the indexes, so the index matters when few rows qualify.</p>

   <h3 id="foreign-keys">The foreign key that makes a delete slow</h3>
   <p>A foreign key adds a hidden query. When a customer is deleted, the database must check that no order still points at it, which searches orders for that customer. Without an index on <code>orders.customer_id</code> that check is a full scan, on every delete. Deleting a customer with no orders read 3 index pages with the index and all 1,725 table pages without it, to find nothing. The cost grows with the child table, so it creeps up unnoticed.</p>
   <p>InnoDB requires such an index and creates one if none exists. PostgreSQL, SQL Server and SQLite leave it to you, and SQLite’s manual calls it “almost always beneficial”. Any index led by the foreign key columns does the job, so the <code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC)</code> index of the order list also serves the check, and a separate single-column index beside it would be redundant.</p>

   <h3 id="innodb-locks">Missing indexes and InnoDB locks</h3>
   <p>In InnoDB a missing index costs locks as well as pages. A locking read, <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code> locks every index record it scans, so one whose <code>WHERE</code> clause has no fitting index locks every row of the table, MySQL’s manual warns, and blocks other users’ inserts until the transaction ends.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="paging">
   <h2>Paging through an ordered list</h2>
   <p>A page of a feed is the order list again, with a tie-breaker so the order is total: an index on <code>(placed_at DESC, order_id DESC)</code> for every order, or the same two columns after <code>customer_id</code> for one customer. With that index the first page of 20 read 4 pages.</p>
   <p>How deeper pages behave depends on the method. An offset page still walks every skipped entry, so page 1,000 read 654 pages, while a keyset page starts from the last key seen and read 4 at any depth. <a href={`${pagination}#page-cost`}>Pagination and large results</a> works through the mechanics: why offset grows, how a keyset condition must be written to reach the index, and the traps of mixed directions and nullable sort columns.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="shapes">
   <h2>Shapes that recur</h2>

   <h3 id="queue">A queue of work</h3>
   <p>Workers repeatedly claim the oldest job in the <code>queued</code> state, while almost every row is <code>done</code> and never read again. A <a href={`${indexes}#partial`}>partial index</a> holds only the queued jobs:</p>
   <Sql code={queueIndex} label="An index that holds only the jobs waiting to run" />
   <Sql code={claim} label="Claim the oldest queued job" />
   <p>With 600 queued jobs among 60,000, the claim read 3 pages through the partial index and 4 through a full index on <code>(state, created_at)</code>. The difference is size and write cost: 4 pages against 234, and the partial index changes only when a job enters or leaves the queue.</p>
   <p>The query must imply the index’s condition: <code>state &lt;&gt; 'done'</code> is close but not the same, and read 1,164 pages. The condition also has to be a literal, as <a href="#parameters">Plans made for parameters</a> explains: in SQL Server a parameter rules out the filtered index on every execution, not only once the plan goes generic. MySQL has no partial indexes, so use a full index led by <code>state</code>. Several workers claiming at once also need <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code>, which PostgreSQL and MySQL support, or SQL Server’s <code>READPAST</code> hint, so that they don’t queue behind each other’s locks.</p>

   <h3 id="soft-delete">Uniqueness among the live rows</h3>
   <p>A table that marks rows deleted instead of removing them breaks the usual unique constraint: a deleted account’s email can’t be reused. A partial unique index covers only the live rows:</p>
   <Sql code={softDelete} label="A unique index that ignores deleted rows" />
   <p>An account marked deleted let a new account take its address, and a third insert with the address in another case failed, because the live row already held it. SQLite supports partial unique indexes, and SQL Server has filtered unique indexes; its case-insensitive default collation makes <code>lower()</code> unnecessary there. MySQL has neither, so the usual workaround is a generated column that is NULL for deleted rows, with a unique index on it, since a unique index allows many NULLs.</p>

   <h3 id="case">Case-insensitive lookup</h3>
   <p>In PostgreSQL and SQLite with default settings, <code>email = 'Shopper0042@Example.com'</code> is case-sensitive. The PostgreSQL answer is an expression index on <code>lower(email)</code> and queries that say <code>lower(email) = ...</code> exactly. Run <code>ANALYZE</code> after creating it: PostgreSQL collects statistics on an indexed expression only then, and until it does the planner guesses how many rows match.</p>
   <p>In SQLite, declare the column or the index <code>COLLATE NOCASE</code>, which folds ASCII letters only. MySQL’s default collation, <code>utf8mb4_0900_ai_ci</code>, and SQL Server’s usual defaults already compare without case, so a plain index serves the lookup; apply a function to the column there and you lose it.</p>

   <h3 id="optional-filters">A search screen with optional filters</h3>
   <p>A search screen lets users filter orders by any mix of customer, status and date. One query that handles every mix is tempting:</p>
   <Sql code={searchAll} label="One query for every combination of filters" />
   <p>It returns the right rows, and it plans badly. A plan that must work whatever the parameters can’t use the customer index, because when <code>$1</code> is NULL the condition matches every row. Reduced to its first filter, as a prepared statement:</p>
   <Sql code={catchAll} label="The one-filter version as a prepared statement" />
   <p>PostgreSQL’s generic plan for <code>orders_for(42)</code> scanned all 1,725 pages. A custom plan, made for the value 42, simplified the condition and read 62. In the default mode PostgreSQL kept choosing custom plans in our runs, even after five executions with NULL, because a custom plan is never costlier than the generic one here. The catch-all then costs a new plan on every execution, and it scans for everyone if <code>plan_cache_mode</code> is set to <code>force_generic_plan</code>. SQL Server caches one plan for all values, so the catch-all usually scans for everyone; <code>OPTION (RECOMPILE)</code> fixes it at the cost of a compile per run.</p>
   <p>The durable fix in every engine is to build the SQL from the filters actually given, leaving the others out, with parameters for the values; in SQL Server, run it with <code>sp_executesql</code>. Each combination then gets its own plan. Serve the common combinations with composite indexes, and let the engine combine single-column indexes for the rest: PostgreSQL’s bitmap scans and MySQL’s index merge can, while SQLite uses one index per table.</p>
   <p>A composite reads fewer pages, but only for its own combination, and only it can supply an order. When the filters become free text and ranking, a <a href="/learn/databases/search/">search system</a> should take over.</p>

  </section>

  <section id="run">
   <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
   <p>The diagnosis numbers, from the bare-column table to the stale statistics and the ORDER BY trap, come from the experiment behind <a href={indexes}>Indexes</a>: PGlite 0.5.8, a WebAssembly build of PostgreSQL 18.3, with every number checked by a script. The rest of the page counts were taken on PostgreSQL 18.6 running natively, with an English ICU collation. The orders table and its pages are the same in both. A plan that also sorts or reads a small table reports a few more pages than the table’s 1,725, which is why some totals read 1,728 or 1,732.</p>
   <p><a href={experimentUrl} download="indexing-queries-experiment.sql">Download the patterns experiment</a> and run it with <code>psql -f indexing-queries-experiment.sql</code> in a scratch database. The diagnosis queries are in the <a href={indexesExperimentUrl} download="index-experiment.sql">Indexes experiment script</a>, which names the table <code>index_orders</code>. All counts are pages, not timings.</p>
   <p>No InnoDB or SQL Server measurement was taken. What this article says about MySQL 8.4 and SQL Server comes from their documentation. SQLite plans were read with <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> in SQLite 3.51.0.</p>
  </section>

